{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Who Counts as Unemployed?","claim":"The title frames unemployment as a classification with criteria for who counts; it does not assert a specific criterion.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Explain who is counted as unemployed—and therefore included in the unemployment-rate denominator—through two contrasting situations: a temporarily laid-off person expecting recall and someone who only browsed job listings.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title accurately introduces the source's classification criteria without adding a factual detail.","materiality":"Low; it establishes the subject."},{"excerpt":"The unemployment rate is calculated using the number of unemployed people divided by the combined number of employed and unemployed people—not everyone who lacks a job.","claim":"The unemployment rate uses unemployed people divided by employed plus unemployed people, not all people without jobs.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"Establish that the unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the combined number of employed and unemployed people, rather than by everyone without a job.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The numerator and denominator, and the contrast with everyone lacking a job, match the source.","materiality":"High; this is the central definition of the rate's denominator."},{"excerpt":"Being jobless alone is not enough to be classified as unemployed.","claim":"Lacking a job by itself does not satisfy the source's definition of unemployed.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The reader should understand that unemployment is not simply the absence of a job: classification depends on being available and, in most cases, actively searching during the specified period.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source requires additional conditions beyond not being employed.","materiality":"High; it distinguishes unemployment from simply having no job."},{"excerpt":"A person must not have been employed during the survey’s reference week and must have been available for work, except in the case of temporary illness.","claim":"A person must not have been employed during the reference week and must have been available for work, with temporary illness as an exception to the availability condition.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness","narrative":"the person must not have been employed during the reference week and must have been available for work, except for temporary illness.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The conditions and the temporary-illness exception match the source.","materiality":"High; these are core classification conditions."},{"excerpt":"In most cases, the person must also have actively searched for work during the four weeks ending with that reference week.","claim":"In the usual case, active job search must occur during the four weeks ending with the reference week.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"Active search must fall within the four-week period ending with the reference week, unless the person is temporarily laid off and expects recall.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The time window is accurate, and “in most cases” reflects the temporary-layoff exception stated in the source.","materiality":"High; it specifies the relevant search period and exception."},{"excerpt":"There is an exception for someone on temporary layoff who expects to be recalled.","claim":"A person temporarily laid off and expecting recall is excepted from the active-search requirement.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source and narrative both identify temporary layoff with expected recall as the active-search exception.","materiality":"High; it changes which people can qualify without searching."},{"excerpt":"That person may qualify as unemployed without actively searching, provided the other conditions are met.","claim":"A temporarily laid-off person expecting recall may qualify without active search, while remaining subject to the other stated conditions.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall.","narrative":"The first may qualify without an active search","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source presents temporary layoff and expected recall as an alternative to active search within the broader unemployment definition; the narrative gives the same exception.","materiality":"High; it clarifies the scope of the exception."},{"excerpt":"By contrast, someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings has not actively searched and does not qualify on that basis.","claim":"Browsing listings alone does not count as active job search, so it does not satisfy the active-search route to unemployment classification.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Passive-only job search does not count as active search.","narrative":"someone whose only job-search activity was browsing listings. The first may qualify without an active search; passive browsing alone does not meet the active-search requirement.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source says passive-only searching is not active; “on that basis” appropriately limits the claim to the active-search route.","materiality":"High; it distinguishes active search from passive browsing."},{"excerpt":"These conditions determine whether a person is counted among the unemployed—and included in the unemployment rate’s denominator.","claim":"The stated classification conditions determine whether a person is counted as unemployed and thus included in the rate's denominator.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Under the Current Population Survey, an unemployed person was not employed during the survey reference week, was available for work except for temporary illness, and made an active job search effort in the four-week period ending with that week or was temporarily laid off and expected recall. The unemployment rate divides unemployed people by the sum of employed and unemployed people.","narrative":"Clarify that availability and the survey reference periods also matter, and that the classification determines whether a person is counted among the unemployed in the rate’s denominator.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The conditions define who is counted as unemployed, and unemployed people are included in the denominator specified by the source.","materiality":"High; it connects the eligibility rules to the rate's denominator."}]}

